Evaluating scholarly output is central to academic resource allocation, yet the profession remains divided between metrics based strictly on journal prestige and those based on citation impact. We develop a unified axiomatic framework that formally integrates ordinal journal tiers with cardinal citation counts. Within this framework, we characterize two complementary families of scholar-ranking rules: Bean-Counting (BC) family that subject journal placements to explicit citation thresholds, and Asymmetric Constant Elasticity of Substitution (CES) aggregators that reward publication breadth within recognized journals and citation depth outside them. An empirical application to a diverse dataset of academic economists demonstrates that these two rule classes capture fundamentally distinct dimensions of research impact and exhibit differential robustness to field-specific citation rescaling.
